<p>Uri stood apart because the cold lasted for days and coincided with widespread power outages across Texas. Without heat, interior temperatures dropped, and pipes that normally survive a brief overnight freeze did not. Many homeowners discovered breaks only when temperatures rose and water started flowing again.</p>

<h2>How burst pipes cause damage</h2>

<p>When water freezes, it expands. In a pipe, that pressure builds between the ice blockage and a closed faucet until the pipe splits or a fitting fails. The break often happens during the freeze, but the leak usually appears during the thaw. That is why damage can show up hours or even days after the coldest night.</p>

<p>Common trouble spots in San Antonio homes include:</p>
<ul>
<li>Supply lines in attics, especially above garages and over exterior walls.</li>
<li>Pipes in exterior walls and under sinks on outside walls.</li>
<li>Hose bibs and backflow assemblies for irrigation systems.</li>
<li>Water heaters and lines in garages or attics.</li>
<li>Crawlspace plumbing in older pier-and-beam homes, such as those in historic neighborhoods like King William, Monte Vista, and Tobin Hill.</li>
<li>Fire sprinkler lines in commercial buildings and some multifamily properties.</li>
</ul>

<figure><img src="/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Public-Adjuster-Pros-and-Cons-1024x683.webp" alt="Couple meeting with an adjuster at a kitchen table with a laptop" width="1024" height="683" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>Walking through the damage, the timeline, and the policy together helps organize a freeze claim.</figcaption></figure>

<h2>Ice storms: roofs, trees, and power lines</h2>

<p>Not every winter claim starts with plumbing. San Antonio's ice storms tend to be light by northern standards, measured in tenths of an inch, but even a quarter inch of ice adds a lot of weight to tree limbs. Live oaks and other evergreen trees hold ice on their leaves, and limbs can break and fall onto roofs, carports, fences, and vehicles. Ice can also pull gutters loose and damage service lines to the house.</p>

<p>When a limb punctures a roof, cold rain or melting ice can enter the attic and ceilings, so a single event can involve roof damage, interior water damage, and debris removal at once. Photograph the limb where it landed before it is cut up, note which structure it hit, and keep the tree service invoice. Many policies cover damage to the structure and some debris removal, often with a sub-limit for trees. If power lines are down, stay clear and report them to CPS Energy.</p>

