<h3>Heat and vacancy conditions</h3>

<p>Many property policies limit coverage for freeze damage when a building is vacant or unoccupied, unless the owner used reasonable care to maintain heat or shut off the water supply and drain the system. This matters for rental properties, homes between tenants, homes for sale, and seasonal properties. A widespread power outage, like the one during Uri, may raise its own questions. Read the exact wording in your policy, since the outcome can turn on it.</p>

[[cta]]

<h2>What to do when a pipe bursts</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Shut off the water</strong> at the main valve. Know where it is before the next freeze. If you cannot find or turn it, contact SAWS or your water provider for help with the meter shutoff.</li>
<li><strong>Turn off electricity</strong> to wet areas if it is safe to do so.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph and video everything</strong> before moving or removing anything: the break, standing water, wet ceilings, damaged floors, and belongings.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the broken pipe section</strong> if a plumber removes it, and ask the plumber to note the cause on the invoice.</li>
<li><strong>Start drying immediately.</strong> In San Antonio's humidity, moisture can lead to mold quickly. A water mitigation company can document moisture readings as it dries the structure.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and write down the claim number and every contact.</li>
<li><strong>Do not throw away damaged items</strong> until they have been photographed and listed, and ask the adjuster whether they need to see them.</li>
<li><strong>Keep every receipt</strong>: plumber, mitigation, hotel, meals, and supplies.</li>
</ol>

<p>Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> walks through what to gather.</p>

<h2>Common disputes in freeze claims</h2>

<h3>"The leak was long-term"</h3>

<p>When stains or rot appear, insurers sometimes argue the leak existed before the freeze. The freeze dates in the NOAA record, a plumber's report describing a split pipe, and photos taken soon after discovery help show that the damage followed the cold.</p>

<h3>Scope that misses hidden damage</h3>

<p>Water travels. It runs along framing, soaks insulation, wicks up drywall, and spreads under flooring and cabinets. An estimate that covers only the visibly wet area may miss damage in adjoining rooms. Moisture mapping and a room-by-room estimate can capture the full scope.</p>

<h3>Flooring and cabinet matching</h3>

<p>When water ruins flooring in one room and the same flooring runs continuously through the house, or when lower cabinets are damaged and uppers cannot be matched, matching questions come up. The answer depends on the policy and the facts, and it is worth raising with documentation.</p>

<h3>Depreciation and contents</h3>

<p>Under many policies, the insurer pays actual cash value first and releases depreciation after repairs or replacement. Our guide to <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains how that works.</p>

<h3>Rental and commercial properties</h3>

<p>Landlords and business owners face extra issues after a freeze. Vacant units, closed offices, and buildings shut down over a weekend can go days before anyone notices a break. Commercial policies may include specific conditions for fire sprinkler systems, protective safeguards, and vacancy, and business income coverage may depend on how long the property is closed for repairs. Tenants' belongings are usually covered by the tenant's own renters policy, not the owner's. Read the full policy, including endorsements, and see our page on <a href="/services/commercial-property-claims/">commercial property claims</a> for more on how these losses are handled.</p>

<h3>How long the insurer has to respond</h3>

<p>Texas Insurance Code Chapter 542 sets general timelines. An insurer generally has 15 days after notice of a claim to acknowledge it, begin its investigation, and request information, then 15 business days after receiving what it asked for to accept or reject the claim. It may notify you that it needs more time, up to 45 additional days. Payment is generally due within 5 business days after the insurer says it will pay. These deadlines are extended by 15 days after a weather-related catastrophe or major natural disaster declared by the commissioner, which can apply after a statewide freeze. Keep a written log of every contact so you can track the timeline.</p>

<h2>Preparing for the next freeze</h2>

<ul>
<li>Insulate exposed pipes in attics, garages, and crawlspaces.</li>
<li>Disconnect hoses and cover hose bibs and irrigation backflow devices.</li>
<li>Open cabinet doors on exterior walls and let faucets drip during hard freezes.</li>
<li>Know where your main shutoff is and keep any needed tool nearby.</li>
<li>If you leave town, keep the heat on or shut off and drain the water, and check your policy's vacancy conditions.</li>
<li>Take dated photos of your home's interior before winter.</li>
</ul>

<h2>When to get help</h2>

<p>A small leak under a sink is often simple. A multi-room loss with ceilings down, flooring ruined, and the family in a hotel is not. San Antonio Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps homeowners and businesses with <a href="/services/freeze-damage-claims/">freeze damage claims</a> and <a href="/services/water-damage-claims/">water damage claims</a> across San Antonio and Bexar County, including documenting hidden damage, preparing a detailed estimate, and presenting the claim. If an insurer has already denied or underpaid a freeze claim, see our pages on <a href="/services/denied-insurance-claims/">denied claims</a> and <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claims</a>. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Outcomes depend on the policy and the facts.</p>

<p>If water came from outside, such as rising water during a storm, read our guide to <a href="/flash-flood-damage-insurance-san-antonio/">flash flood damage and insurance in San Antonio</a> instead, since flood is handled very differently.</p>
